e unlock attempts. The contradiction requires deeper investigation into the Amlogic implementation. Examining the U-Boot source code reveals a crucial `unlock_ability` flag within the `LockData_t info` structure that may be the root cause. This flag's state needs to be verified.\n\n\nThe `info` structure in the Amlogic implementation holds the key: the `unlock_ability` flag directly governs the \"locked device\" error. Although `unlock_critical_state` shows the critical partition is unlocked, the `unlock_ability` flag must also be set to allow full unlocking. The discrepancy between the successful `unlock_critical` and the failed standard unlock is now explained by this crucial flag's state. Investigation will focus on how this flag is set and whether it is being correctly manipulated during the unlock process within the U-Boot environment.\n\n\nThe U-Boot source reveals that, in the ZTE implementation, `cb_oem_cmd` rejects `setenv` commands when the lock state is active, confirmed by the check in `check_lock()`. The `fastboot flashing unlock` operation is contingent on an `unlock_ability` flag. The contradiction of successful unlocks with later failures, despite expected unlock states, raises serious questions about how the unlock operations are actually working, especially over USB. The `B866V2` case demands re-examination of all potential USB communication methods for interactions that unlock the device.\n\n\nAmlogic's `update.exe` in WorldCup/`v2_burning` mode, executed before fastboot initialization, bypasses the lock state checks in `f_fastboot.c` because it runs directly in U-Boot. The possibility of direct transitions between fastboot and the update mode needs confirmation. The decompressed U-Boot binary from `bootloader.img` will be examined to understand command restrictions when a lock is active. The internal storage of U-Boot within the FIP will be investigated to account for previous failed attempts to locate compression.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"r